# 1Password value resolution for legal-gazette-api — LOCAL DEVELOPMENT ONLY.
#
# NOTE: decorator names must never appear with a leading at-sign in prose here.
# Varlock parses every at-token in a comment block as a decorator, so a sentence
# mentioning one is read as a redeclaration with the sentence as its value.
#
# WHAT THIS IS FOR
#
# apps/legal-gazette-api/.env.schema declares WHAT this app needs. This file says WHERE the
# values come from, and exists only so the plugin never appears in a schema a
# container loads. Deployed containers run varlock against the app schema alone,
# where ECS has already filled process.env; they must not drag in the 1Password
# SDK to do it. Verified: registering the plugin upstream instead makes every
# importing app fail with "ref(): invalid dependency: OP_TOKEN".
#
# TWO SOURCES, DELIBERATELY
#
# The rule for deciding where a variable belongs is what the DEPLOYED services
# do, not what local development happens to tolerate:
#
#   differs between deployed apps  ->  that app's own environment
#   identical for every deployed app  ->  shared
#
# So the whole database connection is per-app, not just DB_NAME: Legal Gazette
# and Directorate of Equality run against different databases in production, on
# different hosts with different credentials. The identity server url is shared,
# because every app points at the same one.
#
# The ECS task definitions in the infrastructure repo are the arbiter when it is
# not obvious. If infra sets a variable per service with different values, it is
# per-app here too; local development should mirror that shape rather than
# invent its own.
#
# This is also the whole reason configuration resolves per app rather than being
# exported into the shell: a shell holds one DB_NAME, and we need five.
#
# KEEP THE TWO ENVIRONMENTS DISJOINT, and do not rely on the app environment
# overriding a shared key. Which one wins is still UNVERIFIED: an attempt to
# measure it on legal-gazette-web was inconclusive, because both environments
# turned out to hold the same value for the keys under test, so neither
# disabling one nor swapping the order changed the result.
#
# To give one app its own value, DELETE the key from the shared environment
# rather than shadowing it. That is safe for apps that have not migrated yet:
# they read the gitignored secrets file out of the shell, not this environment.
#
# When a value looks wrong, find out WHERE it came from before changing
# anything, by commenting out one of the two lines above and reloading. Values
# duplicated across both environments make every other diagnosis misleading.
#
# USAGE
#
#   varlock run --path config/1password/legal-gazette-api -- nx serve legal-gazette-api
#
# Values land in that process only. Nothing is exported into your shell, so
# ad-hoc commands (migrations, seeds) need the same wrapper.
#
# Check it with:
#   yarn varlock load --path config/1password/legal-gazette-api --show-all
#
# NOTE ON CACHING: cacheTtl is deliberately NOT set here. It looks like the
# obvious optimisation -- avoid a 1Password round trip on every app launch --
# but varlock's value cache is ENCRYPTED, so every run has to decrypt it, and
# each decrypt asks for biometric unless the encryption daemon happens to be
# holding a session. With cacheTtl=1h set, every single app launch prompted.
# Going without it is quieter: the 1Password desktop integration authorises per
# application rather than per run.
#
# If values ever look stale, VARLOCK_FRESH=1 forces a clean resolve.
#
# @defaultRequired=infer
# @plugin(@varlock/1password-plugin)
# @initOp(token=$OP_TOKEN, allowAppAuth=true)
# @setValuesBulk(opLoadEnvironment($OP_SHARED_ENVIRONMENT))
# @setValuesBulk(opLoadEnvironment($OP_APP_ENVIRONMENT))
# @import(../../../apps/legal-gazette-api/)
# ---

# 1Password service-account token. Leave empty to authenticate through the
# desktop app, which is the normal path on a developer machine. Desktop auth
# connects as YOU, with your full access rather than a scoped service account —
# fine for dev secrets, not for anything else.
# @type=opServiceAccountToken @internal
OP_TOKEN=

# Environment identifiers. Committed on purpose: an environment id is an
# identifier, not a credential, and is useless without authentication — the same
# category as the vault name in an op:// reference. Committing them keeps
# onboarding zero-config instead of making every developer configure twelve ids.
# @public @internal
OP_SHARED_ENVIRONMENT=xg6t32oousqpfj5lbmj4gpjxqe

# Holds only the values that differ for this app.
# @public @internal
OP_APP_ENVIRONMENT=6oej5f7as5etb7evb4txayymrq
